        {
          "secNumber": "2.3",
          "heading": "Personas: evidence-based patterns rather than fictional demographics",
          "content": "### Construct behavioral profiles carefully\n\nA persona is a compact representation of a meaningful pattern of user goals, behaviors, constraints, and context. It can help a team remember whom a design serves, but it is easy to misuse. Giving a fictional character a name, age, photograph, and favorite coffee does not make the persona evidence-based. If the research suggests that some students reserve rooms well in advance while others need a space immediately between classes, the meaningful distinction is planning horizon. Age or major may be irrelevant. The persona should center on the variables that predict design needs, and it should cite the interviews or observations that support each claim.\n\nA defensible profile might be *the time-constrained coordinator*: frequently organizes short group sessions, uses a phone while moving between classes, needs reliable capacity information, and has little time to recover from a failed booking. Another could be *the equipment-dependent researcher*: books less often, requires a projector and accessible entry, and can plan ahead but cannot substitute an arbitrary room. These are provisional patterns, not universal personality types. The team should include evidence strength, relevant counterexamples, and a note about which segments were underrepresented in recruitment. A persona is a working model to be revised, not a permanent identity assigned to users.\n\nPersonas become harmful when teams infer needs from stereotypes. An assumption that older staff dislike technology or that first-year students never read instructions may lead to exclusionary design. Ask whether the attribute was observed, whether it matters to the task, and whether it is stable across contexts. People can move between behavioral modes. A careful planner becomes an urgent seeker when a meeting is rescheduled. A screen-reader user may also be an expert who uses keyboard shortcuts more efficiently than a novice sighted user. Design requirements should follow capabilities and tasks rather than broad labels.\n\nAccessibility should not be relegated to a single persona called *the disabled user*. People with disabilities have different goals, tools, and expertise, and many accessibility requirements apply across profiles. Include relevant access needs within the research matrix and across behavioral patterns. For example, a participant who relies on a screen reader may need the room-capacity selector to expose an accessible name and state; a participant with limited dexterity may need generous touch targets and keyboard alternatives. These requirements must also be checked against standards and tested with relevant users. One participant's preference cannot stand in for an entire population.\n\n### Keep a chain of evidence\n\nEach persona claim should be traceable to a coded episode, quotation, observation, or documented limitation. A practical evidence table contains the claim, supporting cases, contradicting cases, confidence, and design implication. *Needs a reliable confirmation before traveling* might be supported by three field observations and contradicted by one participant who routinely walks to the building without booking. The confidence statement should explain why the pattern is useful despite the exception. Avoid replacing this with a fabricated percentage or a pseudo-statistical score. Qualitative evidence can support an actionable design hypothesis without claiming precise prevalence.\n\nWhen two personas lead to the same design requirement, consider whether the distinction is necessary. If both need an accurate availability timestamp, building separate interfaces for them adds complexity without clear benefit. Conversely, if one group can wait for email confirmation while another needs immediate offline proof, a single default flow may be insufficient. Use scenarios to test persona usefulness: place each profile into a concrete task with constraints, then ask whether the proposed design supports that task. If the persona changes no decisions, it may be decorative rather than analytical.\n\nPersonas should be reviewed when new evidence arrives. A launch may reveal users who coordinate reservations on behalf of others, a group omitted from the initial study. That is a reason to revise the model and recruit appropriately, not to force the new users into the closest existing character. Document version dates and unresolved assumptions. A team can also use job stories or task segments instead of personas when behavioral context matters more than stable user categories. The method is a means of making evidence usable, not a required deliverable for every project.",
          "simulations": []
        },
        {
          "secNumber": "2.4",
          "heading": "Task analysis: representing goals, decisions, and recovery",
          "content": "### Decompose a goal without losing its context\n\nTask analysis describes what people are trying to achieve and the actions, decisions, and information required along the way. Start with a goal such as *secure an appropriate study room for a group of four*. A high-level decomposition might include identifying time constraints, finding candidate rooms, checking capacity and equipment, submitting a request, verifying confirmation, and communicating the location to the group. This decomposition is not yet an interface specification. The existing process might use email, a noticeboard, and conversation rather than a single app. Good task analysis captures that reality before suggesting screens.\n\nA hierarchical task analysis can express alternative plans. Task 0 is *secure a room*. Subtask 1 establishes date, time, group size, and accessibility needs. Subtask 2 checks availability. Subtask 3 compares candidate rooms. Subtask 4 reserves or contacts a coordinator. Subtask 5 confirms the outcome. A plan might say: perform 1\u20133; if an acceptable room exists, perform 4\u20135; otherwise revise the time or use an alternative location. This conditional structure is important. A linear list that ignores the no-room-available branch will produce a brittle implementation and misleading usability tasks.\n\nDistinguish user goals from system actions. *Understand whether the room is accessible* is a goal; *open the accessibility tab* is one possible interface action. A new design may satisfy the goal by placing access details directly in search results, eliminating the tab. If task analysis begins with the current button sequence, it can accidentally preserve avoidable friction. Record information dependencies: to compare two rooms, a user needs comparable capacity, location, equipment, availability, and booking conditions. Inconsistent units or missing labels can make the comparison impossible even when every button functions correctly.\n\nExceptions deserve explicit treatment. What if a booking request times out after payment or confirmation? What if another person reserves the room before the user finishes? What if the user cannot authenticate while offline? For each failure, specify what the user knows, what the system knows, and which recovery options are available. A safe workflow avoids silent duplicate reservations and exposes the status of pending requests. Task analysis can reveal that *confirm reservation* is not a single click but a state transition that needs idempotent handling, an audit trail, and clear feedback. The technical solution should follow the identified risk, not be imposed before research.\n\n### Estimate effort and identify leverage points\n\nTime-on-task can be decomposed into stages, but the result must preserve uncertainty. Suppose an observed attempt takes 20 seconds to find the entry point, 35 seconds to compare rooms, 15 seconds to submit, and 40 seconds to confirm with teammates. Total elapsed time is 110 seconds. A prototype that reduces submission time to 8 seconds saves only 7 seconds if every other stage is unchanged. Improving confirmation may matter more than optimizing the submit button. This simple arithmetic prevents teams from concentrating on the most visible screen while ignoring the largest bottleneck.\n\nA task model should include error cost as well as time. Accidentally booking a room at the wrong campus can be more consequential than spending ten extra seconds comparing options. Define success criteria from the user's perspective: the reservation is valid, the room fits the group, accessibility requirements are met, and the confirmation is understandable. Count errors such as selecting the wrong date or overlooking equipment, but also note near misses and recoveries. A user who notices a mistake and corrects it before submission has completed the task, yet the interface may still need improvement.\n\nWhen prioritizing changes, separate frequency, severity, and confidence. A rare but serious accessibility failure can deserve urgent attention even if it appears in only one observed session. A frequent cosmetic annoyance may be lower priority. A high-severity finding based on weak evidence can justify a focused follow-up study before a large redesign. Use a decision log: state the evidence, proposed intervention, expected mechanism, and a measurable test that could disconfirm the hypothesis. That record makes design reasoning inspectable and protects against treating a single workshop opinion as settled research.\n\n### Integrated case: a research-to-design handoff\n\nConsider a fictional campus service team with six interviews, four contextual observations, and an existing booking log. Interviews suggest uncertainty about room availability; observation reveals that students often check messages from classmates; logs show repeated search visits without a confirmed reservation. These sources converge on a possible trust and confirmation problem, but they do not establish that every repeated visit represents failure. The team writes a task model covering discovery, comparison, reservation, and verification, then prototypes a clear status message and a shareable confirmation. It measures whether participants can correctly explain the booking state and whether they send fewer clarification messages in a controlled test.\n\nThe team should recruit participants who differ in planning horizon, accessibility requirements, and familiarity with the service. It should report what was observed and what remains unknown, rather than announcing that the prototype solves the problem before evaluation. The research output includes an interview guide, de-identified field notes, a codebook, an evidence table, a provisional persona set, and a task model with failure branches. None is valuable merely because it exists. Together, they form an auditable chain from user experience to a falsifiable design decision.",
          "simulations": []
        }
